<Layout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Find the parts you need, connect your speaker, and set up OpenAthan’s prayer times and volume."
>
  <article class="docs wrap">
    <div class="docs-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">DOCUMENTATION</p><h1>Getting started</h1><p class="lede">
        The parts you’ll need and the steps to set up your Athan speaker.
      </p>
    </div><div class="docs-body">
      <aside class="notice">
        <strong>In development — first-time installation is not available yet.</strong><p>
          OpenAthan is in early development. The installer will open when tested firmware and
          approved recordings are published. Already have compatible OpenAthan firmware? You can
          <a href="/install/">fix Wi-Fi or your password over USB</a>.
        </p>
      </aside><h2>What you’ll need</h2><p>
        Purchase an <a href="https://docs.m5stack.com/en/core/AtomS3R">M5Stack AtomS3R C126</a>{' '}
        and a <a href="https://docs.m5stack.com/en/atom/Echo_Pyramid">Pyramid A167</a> speaker base separately.
        M5Stack calls the A167 “Echo Pyramid”; OpenAthan also refers to it as Voice Pyramid. These instructions
        and the browser installer currently support only this combination of parts.
      </p><p>
        You will need a computer with desktop Chrome or Edge, a USB-C <strong>data</strong> cable, and
        a 2.4 GHz WPA2/WPA3 personal Wi-Fi network. A charging-only cable will not work for USB setup.
        Enterprise networks and networks with a sign-in page are not supported.
      </p><CableDiagram /><h2>1. Connect through the Atom</h2><p>
        Attach the modules using their intended connector. Disconnect bottom power, then connect the
        computer to the <strong>Atom’s USB-C port</strong>. Use only this one cable during USB
        setup.
      </p><p>
        Close other serial tools and browser tabs using the device. On <a href="/install/"
          >the installer page</a
        >, choose your path and select the USB JTAG/serial device when prompted.
      </p><h2>2. Install, or recover an existing device</h2><p>
        A new installation replaces the device’s existing firmware and data. It is not a
        settings-preserving update. If OpenAthan is already installed, choose <strong
          >Fix Wi-Fi or password</strong
        >. Recovery preserves your prayer settings and playback history.
      </p><h2>3. Save Wi-Fi and a device password</h2><p>
        Select your home network or enter its name for a hidden network, then enter its Wi-Fi
        password. Next, create a separate, long device password. Your browser sends these
        credentials directly over USB; the website does not store them.
      </p><p>
        Remember the device password. You will use the username <strong>admin</strong> to sign into the
        device’s settings.
      </p><h2>4. Move the power cable</h2><p>
        When USB setup finishes, unplug the Atom’s USB cable. Connect <strong
          >only the Pyramid’s bottom USB-C power</strong
        > for normal speaker operation. Wait for Wi-Fi to reconnect.
      </p><h2>5. Finish on the device</h2><p>
        Keep your computer or phone on the same home network and open the device address shown by
        the installer, such as <code>http://openathan-a1b2c3.local/</code>. Each device has its own
        address. If the hostname does not work, try its displayed IP address.
      </p><p>
        Sign in, enter your location and timezone, choose a calculation method, and review the
        prayer times. Select <strong>Finish setup</strong> to enable the Athan. If the clock has not
        synchronized yet, Athan playback will wait.
      </p><h2>Everyday use</h2><p>
        Your speaker calculates prayer times and plays recordings stored on the device. The current
        build needs internet access to set its clock after a restart, before it can play the Athan.
        To open its settings, connect your phone or computer to the same home Wi-Fi. Keep the
        settings page on your trusted home network; do not make it accessible from the internet.
      </p><div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/install/">Open device setup</a><a href="/docs/troubleshooting/"
          >Troubleshooting</a
        >
      </div>
      <h2 id="other-hardware">Other hardware</h2>
      <p>
        OpenAthan is designed to keep prayer calculations, scheduling, and settings independent of
        the hardware. Hardware adapters connect these functions to a device’s audio and controls,
        allowing contributors to develop lower-cost builds or add capabilities on other hardware.
      </p>
      <p>
        Audio playback is required. Displays, status lights, buttons, touch controls, microphones, a
        hardware clock, and removable storage are optional capabilities. Standalone operation does
        not require Home Assistant.
      </p>
      <p>
        Additional hardware integrations require development and testing. The reusable core and
        ESPHome integration provide a starting point; broader support for optional peripherals is
        still planned. The browser installer currently targets only our first reference build.
      </p>
      <p>For implementation details, see:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/OpenAthan-Project/openathan/blob/main/docs/development/architecture.md"
            >Firmware architecture and hardware adapters</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/OpenAthan-Project/openathan/blob/main/firmware/esphome/scheduler/README.md#controls-and-reuse"
            >Scheduler controls and reuse</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
